ts a later borrowing directly from Old Swedish kyndil (“candle, torch”). Either way, ultimately from Latin candēla (“candle”"},{"parola":"piin","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"sì","motivo_incerta":"Probably; or possibly; either","significato":"pain, anguish, torment, suffering, agony, torture","termine_origine":"pina","catena":[{"codice":"gmq-osw","nome":"Old Swedish","termine":"pina","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"gmq-osw","ambiti":[],"via":"","alternative":[{"codice":"gmq-osw","nome":"Old Swedish","termine":"pina","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"gml","nome":"Middle Low German","termine":"pīn","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"la","nome":"Latin","termine":"poena","categoria":"non_precisato"},{"codice":"grc","nome":"Ancient Greek","termine":"ποινή","traslitterazione":"poinḗ","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"testo":"Derived either from Old Swedish pina, or possibly from Middle Low German pīn, pīne, from Latin poena, from Ancient Greek ποινή (poinḗ). Compare Danish pine; cognate to Finnish piina and Votic piinõ (the latter is probably borrowed from Estonian)."}]}